<chapter xml:id="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Visualising Multidimensional Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-visualising-multidimensional">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding" /> was about trusting your data.
      This chapter is about actually <em>seeing</em> it. A clean, correctly
      coded dataset with more than two or three variables is still, on its
      own, just a table <mdash /> and a table of more than a few hundred
      rows tells a human reader almost nothing. The moment a dataset has
      real structure to it (groups, locations, time, several measurements
      per case) the question stops being <q>is this value right?</q> and
      becomes <q>how do I look at several things at once?</q>
    </p>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: Following the Giants                                  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-following-the-giants">
    <title>Following the Giants</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Between September 2011 and April 2022, researchers working across
        the Greater Mara Ecosystem in Kenya fitted 58 elephants with GPS
        collars recording location roughly every hour. By the time Wall et
        al. (2024) published their analysis, the collars had produced
        1,176,287 raw GPS positions; 49 of the elephants, 26 male and 23
        female, contributed 834,138 of those positions to the final
        analysis. Alongside every location, the data were paired with
        vegetation cover, slope, distance to water, the presence of human
        infrastructure such as roads and agriculture, the season (wet or
        dry), and time of day.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mara-scale">
      <title>Over a million rows is not something anyone reads</title>

      <p>
        Nobody scrolls through 834,138 rows looking for a pattern. At that
        scale, a spreadsheet stops being a way to look at data and becomes
        just a way to store it. Understanding what these elephants were
        doing required combining GPS location with every one of those
        environmental variables at once, across an area spanning both
        protected reserve land and unprotected land under human use <mdash />
        which is exactly the kind of question a table cannot answer and a
        well-built visualisation can.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mara-findings">
      <title>What the combined view showed</title>

      <p>
        Mapping elephant locations against land-use and vegetation layers
        revealed that resource selection <mdash /> which habitats elephants
        actively chose, versus merely passed through <mdash /> differed
        sharply depending on where an elephant was. Elephants showed strong
        selection for closed-canopy forest and consistently avoided open
        grassland, and this avoidance was markedly stronger in unprotected
        areas than inside protected reserves. Male and female elephants
        differed in how they used the landscape, most sharply within the
        Mara Reserve itself. And elephants moving through protected land
        showed far more behavioural flexibility than those constrained by
        human-dominated landscapes outside it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The combined home ranges of the tracked elephants covered 18,758
        square kilometres, spanning both protected and unprotected zones.
        Historically, elephants may have moved regularly between the Mau
        forest and the Mara <mdash /> but across the full eleven years of
        tracking, the researchers found no evidence that this connection
        still exists. Agriculture and fencing, the study concludes, are
        <q>generally considered incompatible with healthy elephant ranging
        behaviour,</q> and it is precisely in the unprotected land between
        reserves, where those pressures are concentrated, that a
        once-plausible corridor has gone quiet.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-elephants-lesson">
        <title>Not more data. The same data, seen jointly.</title>
        <p>
          None of this <mdash /> the forest preference, the sex differences,
          the corridor gone quiet under agricultural pressure <mdash /> is
          visible from any single column of the dataset, or even from
          location data on its own. It only becomes visible once location is
          examined <em>jointly</em> with land cover, protection status, sex
          and season, together, in the same analysis and the same maps. The
          collars did not need to collect more data to reveal this. The data
          needed to be looked at with enough variables in view at once.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <table xml:id="table-mara-dimensions">
        <title>What had to be held in view at once to find these patterns</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Held in view</cell>
            <cell>Variable(s)</cell>
            <cell>What it contributed</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>Position</cell><cell>latitude, longitude</cell><cell>where each elephant actually was</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Land cover</cell><cell>NDVI, vegetation cover</cell><cell>forest vs. grassland preference</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Zone</cell><cell>protected vs. unprotected</cell><cell>where avoidance and plasticity differed</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Individual</cell><cell>sex</cell><cell>where the sharpest behavioural split appeared</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Human pressure</cell><cell>agriculture, fencing, roads</cell><cell>why the Mau<ndash />Mara corridor has gone quiet</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Five things, held in view together, none of them new data collection
        <mdash /> the same shape as every dimension added in
        <xref ref="sec-gapminder" /> below.
      </p>

      <p>
        Throughout this chapter, you will learn how to build exactly that
        kind of view: visualisations that hold several variables in view at
        once, rather than one table or one chart per variable.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Wall, J., Hahn, N., Carroll, S., Mwiu, S.,
        Goss, M., Sairowua, W., Tiedeman, K., Kiambi, S., Omondi, P.,
        Douglas-Hamilton, I. and Wittemyer, G. (2024). <q>Land use drives
        differential resource selection by African elephants in the
        Greater Mara Ecosystem, Kenya.</q> <em>Movement Ecology</em>, 12, 11.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 2: Gapminder                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-gapminder">
    <title>Building Up Dimensions: The Gapminder Activity</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Hans Rosling's Gapminder data make the same point as the elephants,
        with a dataset you can actually hold in your hands. The data are
        completely open, and a cleaned excerpt is bundled directly into R as
        the <c>gapminder</c> package: 1,704 observations covering 142
        countries from 1952 to 2007, with one row per country per year and
        columns for <c>country</c>, <c>continent</c>, <c>year</c>,
        <c>lifeExp</c> (life expectancy), <c>pop</c> (population), and
        <c>gdpPercap</c> (GDP per person).
      </p>

      <p>
        A single row tells you almost nothing interesting. What made
        Rosling's presentation of this data famous was building it up, one
        variable at a time, into a single animated chart <mdash /> the exact
        idea behind <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants" />, with a dataset
        small enough to work through by hand.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-2d">
      <title>Two dimensions: does money buy a longer life?</title>

      <p>
        Start with the most basic possible question: is a country's income
        related to how long its people live? Two variables, two axes.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(gapminder)
        library(ggplot2)

        gapminder_2007 &lt;- subset(gapminder, year == 2007)

        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
      </program>

      <p>
        Even this bare scatterplot shows a clear upward trend, though
        income is logged on the x-axis because a handful of very rich
        countries would otherwise crush the rest of the points into one
        corner. But every point is currently the same colour and the same
        size <mdash /> a Norwegian point and a Nigerian point are visually
        identical except for position.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-3d">
      <title>Three dimensions: adding continent</title>

      <p>
        Map <c>continent</c> onto colour, and a third variable appears in
        the same chart without adding a single new axis.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp, colour = continent)) +
          geom_point() +
          scale_x_log10() +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy")
      </program>

      <p>
        The trend from <xref ref="ss-gapminder-2d" /> is still there, but
        now it has structure: African countries cluster toward the lower
        left, European countries toward the upper right, and the spread
        within each continent becomes visible for the first time. Nothing
        about the underlying data changed. One aesthetic mapping did.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-4d">
      <title>Four dimensions: adding population</title>

      <p>
        Map <c>pop</c> onto point size, and the chart gains a fourth
        variable, turning a scatterplot into a bubble chart.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(gapminder_2007, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                                   colour = continent, size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 15)) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
      </program>

      <p>
        China and India, similar in income and life expectancy to several
        smaller countries, now visibly dominate the chart by sheer size.
        That is real information <mdash /> population <mdash /> that a plain
        scatterplot could not have shown without a second, separate chart.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-5d">
      <title>Five dimensions: adding time</title>

      <p>
        The chart so far is a single snapshot, 2007 only. Rosling's original
        animated the chart across every year in the data, letting countries
        move as their income and life expectancy changed. Without animation
        software, the same fifth dimension <mdash /> time <mdash /> can be
        shown as small multiples, one panel per year.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp,
                              colour = continent, size = pop)) +
          geom_point(alpha = 0.7) +
          scale_x_log10() +
          scale_size_continuous(range = c(1, 10)) +
          facet_wrap(~ year) +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita (log scale)", y = "Life expectancy",
               size = "Population")
      </program>

      <p>
        Read left to right, top to bottom, and entire countries visibly
        migrate across the panel: life expectancy climbing steadily almost
        everywhere, even where income barely moves; the occasional sharp
        reversal, a bubble dropping backward in a single panel, worth
        investigating rather than dismissing as noise. (If you have the
        <c>gganimate</c> package installed, the same five variables can be
        drawn as a single moving chart, <c>transition_time(year)</c>, closer
        to Rosling's original.)
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-gapminder-summary">
      <title>What just happened</title>

      <p>
        Five variables, five aesthetics, and not one extra table.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-gapminder-dimensions">
        <title>Dimensions added to the Gapminder chart, one at a time</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Dimension</cell>
            <cell>Aesthetic</cell>
            <cell>Variable</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>1st &amp; 2nd</cell><cell>x, y position</cell><cell><c>gdpPercap</c>, <c>lifeExp</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>3rd</cell><cell>colour</cell><cell><c>continent</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>4th</cell><cell>size</cell><cell><c>pop</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>5th</cell><cell>facet / animation frame</cell><cell><c>year</c></cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Each new variable was added as a new <term>aesthetic mapping</term>,
        not a new chart, a new axis, or a new table. That is the whole
        technique this chapter is teaching, and <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants" />
        was the same idea before it had a name: GPS location, land cover,
        protection status, sex and season, held together in one analysis
        rather than one table per variable, is what turned 834,138 rows of
        coordinates into a finding about forest preference, sex differences
        and a corridor gone quiet. Five variables again, in substance if not
        in a single ggplot call <mdash /> the same move as
        <xref ref="ss-gapminder-4d" />, just with a resource-selection model
        standing in for <c>colour =</c> and <c>size =</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bryan, J. <c>gapminder</c> R package,
        data curated by the Gapminder Foundation
        (<url href="https://www.gapminder.org" visual="gapminder.org" />),
        based on the work of Hans Rosling.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 3: a short gallery                                       -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-viz-gallery">
    <title>A Short Gallery: When Two Dimensions Weren't Enough</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The elephants and Gapminder are recent examples of a much older
        idea. Long before <c>ggplot2</c>, before spreadsheets, before
        computers, people were solving exactly this problem <mdash /> a
        table or a simple chart that cannot show what actually matters
        <mdash /> by finding a way to hold several variables in one image at
        once. Four examples, spanning more than 150 years, make the case
        better than any single modern one can on its own.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-minard">
      <title>Minard's map of Napoleon's Russian campaign (1869)</title>

      <p>
        Charles Joseph Minard was a retired French civil engineer, 80 years
        old, when he published a single lithograph that Edward Tufte later
        called <q>possibly the best statistical graphic ever drawn.</q> It
        depicts Napoleon's catastrophic 1812 invasion of Russia and the
        retreat that followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        A bar chart of monthly troop losses would need several separate
        charts to show army size, geographic position, direction of travel,
        and the temperature that killed so many soldiers on the retreat.
        Minard fit all of it into one image. A tan band, its width scaled at
        precisely one millimetre per ten thousand men, traces the army's
        advance eastward toward Moscow, drawn directly onto a map so that
        position and army size are shown together. A black band, same
        scale, traces the retreat back. Beneath the map, a separate strip
        plots temperature against date along the retreat route, so the
        collapse in troop numbers can be read directly against the cold that
        caused so much of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Six variables, one image: geography, direction of travel, troop
        numbers, the route itself, temperature, and time. The tan band
        starts near the Polish border at 422,000 men and narrows steadily
        marching into Russia, losing soldiers to disease and combat even
        before Moscow. The retreat band is where the graphic becomes
        devastating: it thins almost to nothing by the time it reaches the
        same border, months later, at around 10,000 survivors, while the
        temperature strip beneath falls below <m>-30^\circ\text{C}</m> at the
        worst of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        A table of monthly casualty figures existed and always existed. It
        could not make the collapse visible the way the combined graphic
        does, because a table cannot show a retreating army's position, its
        shrinking size, and the falling temperature all bearing on each
        other at once, in a single glance.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Minard, C.J. (1869). <em>Carte figurative des
        pertes successives en hommes de l'Armée Française dans la campagne
        de Russie 1812<ndash />1813.</em> Described in Tufte, E.R. (1983).
        <em>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information.</em> Cheshire,
        CT: Graphics Press.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-nightingale">
      <title>Nightingale's Coxcomb (1858)</title>

      <p>
        In 1858, Florence Nightingale published a report built almost
        entirely around one number she needed British officials to stop
        ignoring: of the soldiers who died during the Crimean War, the
        overwhelming majority died not in battle but of preventable disease
        in military hospitals.
      </p>

      <p>
        She could have reported a single national mortality rate. Instead
        she built what became known as the <term>coxcomb</term>, or rose
        diagram: a circular chart of twelve wedges, one per month, each
        wedge's area scaled to that month's deaths, each wedge divided by
        colour into three causes <mdash /> preventable disease, wounds from
        battle, and all other causes. As with Minard's map, the point is not
        one summary number but a pattern developing over time, visible only
        because time, cause, and magnitude are encoded in the same image.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pattern is stark. In the war's first winter, before a Sanitary
        Commission arrived in March 1855 to overhaul hygiene in the camps
        and hospitals, the wedges for disease dwarf the wedges for wounds,
        month after month. After the Commission's reforms, the wedges shrink
        sharply. A table of the same monthly totals contains identical
        information; it does not make the argument nearly as forcefully,
        because a table asks the reader to do the comparing themselves,
        while the coxcomb does the comparing for them, at a glance.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nightingale was a trained statistician petitioning a government that
        had shown little appetite for reading tables of army mortality
        statistics. The diagram is credited as a direct influence on the
        sanitary reforms, in the military and more broadly, that followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Nightingale, F. (1858). <em>Notes on Matters
        Affecting the Health, Efficiency, and Hospital Administration of
        the British Army, Founded Chiefly on the Experience of the Late
        War.</em> London: Harrison and Sons.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-snow">
      <title>John Snow's cholera map (1854)</title>

      <p>
        In late August 1854, cholera broke out in Soho, London, killing over
        600 people within a few weeks, concentrated overwhelmingly around
        Broad Street. Cholera was widely believed at the time to spread
        through <q>miasma,</q> bad air, rather than water; there was no
        accepted mechanism connecting the disease to anything as specific as
        a single pump.
      </p>

      <p>
        The physician John Snow suspected otherwise, and tested it by
        mapping rather than tabulating. On a street plan of the
        neighbourhood, he marked a dash for every recorded death at the
        address where it occurred, and marked every local water pump. The
        map made a pattern visible that a table of addresses and death
        counts could not: deaths clustered tightly around a single pump on
        Broad Street and thinned with distance from it, while households
        with their own water supply, or a nearby brewery whose workers drank
        almost none of the local water, had almost no cases at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Location and death count, combined in a single image, did what
        neither could do alone. Snow presented the map to the local parish
        and persuaded them to remove the handle from the Broad Street pump.
        The outbreak was already declining by that point, so the act did not
        single-handedly end it <mdash /> that specific detail is often
        overstated <mdash /> but the map itself provided the first strong
        evidence that cholera spread through contaminated water rather than
        air, and a later well inspection found the pump's water supply sat
        near a leaking cesspit, connecting the map's pattern to an actual
        physical cause.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Snow, J. (1855). <em>On the Mode of
        Communication of Cholera</em> (2nd ed.). London: John Churchill.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-viz-challenger">
      <title>The Challenger disaster (1986)</title>

      <p>
        On 28 January 1986, the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73
        seconds after launch, killing all seven crew members. The immediate
        cause was the failure of an O-ring seal on a solid rocket booster, a
        component engineers at Morton Thiokol, the manufacturer, had already
        flagged as a concern in cold weather.
      </p>

      <p>
        The night before launch, those engineers argued against flying,
        worried specifically about the forecast temperature. Ambient
        temperature at launch was later measured at 36°F, with the
        temperature at the failed joint itself estimated at 28<ndash />31°F
        <mdash /> roughly 15 degrees colder than any previous launch. Their
        argument did not persuade NASA management, and the launch went
        ahead.
      </p>

      <p>
        Edward Tufte's later analysis, in <em>Visual Explanations</em>
        (1997), argues that part of why the engineers failed to persuade
        anyone was how their evidence was presented. Under time pressure,
        their chart plotted O-ring damage only for flights that had already
        shown some, leaving out every fully successful flight. Displayed
        that way, the relationship between cold and damage does not
        obviously stand out. Tufte reconstructed the same data as a single
        chart of temperature against O-ring damage for <em>every</em>
        flight, successful or not. Plotted that way, the relationship is
        difficult to miss, and the forecast launch temperature sits clearly
        outside the range of every previous flight.
      </p>

      <p>
        This case is genuinely contested, and worth reading that way rather
        than as a clean morality tale. Some historians of the disaster argue
        Tufte's account overstates how much a better chart alone would have
        changed the outcome, given the institutional and schedule pressures
        NASA management was under regardless of what any chart showed, and
        some note that even Tufte's reconstructed chart is not fully
        faithful to the data available that night. What is not contested is
        the underlying point: a chart showing only part of the relevant data
        <mdash /> here, only the flights with problems <mdash /> can hide
        exactly the pattern a reader most needs to see. Showing all of it is
        what makes a relationship visible or invisible.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Presidential Commission on the Space Shuttle
        Challenger Accident (1986). <em>Report to the President.</em>
        Washington, DC: US Government Printing Office. Tufte, E.R. (1997).
        <em>Visual Explanations: Images and Quantities, Evidence and
        Narrative.</em> Cheshire, CT: Graphics Press.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <objectives xml:id="objectives-visualising-multidimensional">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Explain why a large, clean dataset can still be unreadable without visualisation.</p></li>
      <li><p>Add variables to a plot using aesthetic mappings (colour, size, facet) rather than separate tables or charts.</p></li>
      <li><p>Build up a multidimensional visualisation incrementally, one variable at a time, and describe what each addition reveals.</p></li>
      <li><p>Choose an appropriate encoding (colour vs. size vs. facet vs. animation) for a given variable and dataset.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-visualising-multidimensional">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-elephants-variables">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-following-the-giants" /> pairs each elephant's GPS
          location with several other variables: vegetation cover, distance
          to water, human infrastructure, sex, season, and time of day.
          <xref ref="ss-mara-findings" /> describes findings that came from
          examining location jointly with land cover, protection status,
          sex and season. Pick one variable not explicitly named in that
          combination <mdash /> time of day, or distance to water <mdash />
          and describe, in a sentence or two, what mapping it onto colour,
          size, or facet might reveal that the described analysis would not.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-gapminder-1952">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Adapt the code in <xref ref="ss-gapminder-4d" /> to show 1952
          instead of 2007. Compare the two charts. Which continents have
          moved the most in the fifty-five years between them, and along
          which axis?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-flamingo-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> from
          <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" />. Build a single plot that encodes
          at least four variables at once (for example: bill length and body
          mass on the axes, species on colour, lake on facet). What does the
          combined view show that a single-variable summary of each column,
          taken separately, would not?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-viz-too-many-dimensions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-gapminder-5d" /> uses position, colour, size, and
          facet together, four aesthetics beyond the two axes. Is there a
          point at which adding another variable to the same chart would
          make it harder to read rather than easier? Argue for a specific
          upper limit, and say what you would do with a fifth or sixth
          variable instead of cramming it into the same plot.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</chapter>
